-- title:
--   Fibrewise Cartier divisors in a flat family are relative
-- statement:
--   Let $q\colon X\to T$ be a morphism of schemes which is flat and locally of finite type, with $T$ locally Noetherian, let $M$ be an object of $X.\mathrm{Modules}$ which is invertible in the sense that every point of $X$ has an open neighbourhood $U$ over which the pullback of $M$ along $U\hookrightarrow X$ is isomorphic to the unit sheaf of modules on $U$, and let $s\colon \mathbf 1 \to M$ be a morphism from the monoidal unit, i.e. a global section of $M$. Write $Z(s)$ for the ideal sheaf datum `zeroSchemeIdeal s`, the infimum of all ideal sheaf data $J$ on $X$ with $\mathrm{coeffIdeal}\,s\,U \le J.\mathrm{ideal}\,U$ for every affine open $U$, where $\mathrm{coeffIdeal}\,s\,U \subseteq \Gamma(X,U)$ is the ideal generated by the range of the coefficient map $\mathrm{coeff}\,s\,U$ (the values of $s$ read off through trivialisations of $M$ over $U$). Assume that for every algebraically closed field $k$ and every morphism $x\colon \operatorname{Spec} k \to T$ the inverse image of $Z(s)$ along the projection $X\times_T \operatorname{Spec} k \to X$ is invertible, in the sense that each of its points lies in a basic open $D(f)$ of some affine open $U$ on which the ideal is generated by a single non-zerodivisor of $\Gamma$ of that basic open. Then $Z(s)$ itself is invertible in this same sense, and the composite of the closed immersion of the subscheme cut out by $Z(s)$ with $q$ is flat.
--
--   This is the criterion that a section of a line bundle whose zero scheme is an effective Cartier divisor on every geometric fibre of a flat, locally finite type family over a locally Noetherian base cuts out a relative effective Cartier divisor, flat over the base (EGA IV, 11.3.8); no properness or finiteness of the zero scheme over $T$ is asserted. It is used in the construction of relative effective Cartier divisors from ideal sheaves, via [`AlgebraicGeometry.RelEffCartierDiv.exists_I_eq_zeroSchemeIdeal`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.RelEffCartierDiv.exists_I_eq_zeroSchemeIdeal) and its variant for ideals supported in a given closed set.
